clearhead-core/crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/action.rs:222: blocking: 4#1 complete/cancel select the target with find_best_match(..., |_| true), and find_act_in_open_files (line 1168, used by update) does the same; reopen at line 608 has the mirror problem. Before, the selection filtered with is_open_action. With closed lines now staying in the active file, a name or substring query matches closed lines: the first match wins, or the query becomes ambiguous. — Reproduced: with [x] Water plants and [ ] Water plants in one file, `clearhead complete action "Water plants"` fails with already-closed and closes nothing; `complete action Draft` picks a closed 'Draft report' over the open 'Draft report v2'. Materialized occurrences share their master's name, so this becomes the common case. Selection should prefer lines in the verb's source state (open for close and update, closed for reopen) and fall back to all lines only to report AlreadyClosed or AlreadyOpen.
clearhead-core/crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/action_lifecycle.rs:20: should-fix: 4#2 The single-close path is still duplicated. prepare_close_action_subtree and prepare_reopen_action_subtree are public Core API, named 'subtree', and call prepare_transaction without occurrences, so they close without resolving. The filesystem close_action_subtree and reopen_action_subtree (clearhead-cli/src/filesystem/mod.rs:216, 269) duplicate transition_action. prepare_action_archive duplicates prepare_action_move. close_subtree and reopen_subtree (domain/mod.rs:721, 766) are still exported though only tests call them. — The proposal says resolving belongs to the one shared close and that close_subtree/reopen_subtree go once nothing calls them. A public close that skips occurrence resolution is the exact gap this work closes, and parallel planners drift apart.
